IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cor-dates`
IMPORT `cor-rules`

-- Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) -- the questions of the FAMILY MEMBER of a person who has died: the senior next of kin, another
-- member of the immediate family, or another person with an interest in the death (a person who reported it, asked for
-- an inquest, applied for the body, or was given leave to take part). Some questions (ss 42, 50, 57) are asked by any
-- person the coroner calls on, as a witness or for a document. The Act's terms with plain glosses; the coroner's and the
-- courts' judgments are inputs. Each question takes only plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list); the
-- two duties with deadlines (ss 42(3), 52(6)) are DEONTIC rules run inside, from cor-rules.l4.
--
-- Where a section is used by one question only, its words are applied here, with the quotation; the shared definitions
-- (ss 3, 4, 5), the regulative rules and the Part 7 table are in cor-rules.l4.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Coroners Act
-- 2008, Version No. 046 (1 November 2026), not the authorised version. (c) State of Victoria; published with the
-- authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be reproduced except in accordance with the
-- Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Coroners Act 2008 -- the family's questions`

§§ `Who you are to the person who died`

@export Am I the "senior next of kin" of the person who died (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 3)? The senior next of kin is told of an autopsy direction and may object, is told of an exhumation, may ask for the medical reports, may apply to re-open or set aside findings, and may appeal several decisions.
GIVEN `what is my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: their spouse or domestic partner immediately before death, son or daughter, parent (if they were under 18 this includes a step-, adoptive or foster parent, guardian or person with their daily care), sibling (including half-, adoptive and step-siblings), the executor named in their will, their personal representative immediately before death, or none of these
      `am I 18 or over` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are 18 or over (it matters for a son, daughter or sibling)
      `is a spouse or partner available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person had a spouse or domestic partner immediately before death who is available (answer no if they had none)
      `is an adult son or daughter available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a son or daughter of the person who is 18 or over is available
      `is a parent available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a parent of the person is available
      `is an adult sibling available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a sibling of the person who is 18 or over is available
      `is an executor available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person named in the will as an executor is available
      `is a personal representative available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person who was the deceased's personal representative immediately before the death is available
      `has the coroner determined it is me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has determined that you should be taken to be the senior next of kin because of the closeness of your relationship with the person (s 3(3))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Am I the senior next of kin` MEANS
    `s 3 -- I am the senior next of kin` `what is my relationship` `am I 18 or over` `is a spouse or partner available` `is an adult son or daughter available` `is a parent available` `is an adult sibling available` `is an executor available` `is a personal representative available` `has the coroner determined it is me`

@export Am I a member of the "immediate family" of the person who died (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 3)? It matters for reporting a death after a mental health discharge (s 12(2)).
GIVEN `what is my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse or domestic partner, son or daughter, parent, sibling, executor named in the will, personal representative, or none of these
      `has the coroner determined it is me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has determined you to be the senior next of kin because of the closeness of your relationship (s 3(3))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Am I immediate family` MEANS
    `s 3 -- I am immediate family` `what is my relationship` `has the coroner determined it is me`

-- s 3(1) "domestic partner of a person means -- (a) a person who is in a registered relationship with the person; or (b)
-- an adult person to whom the person is not married but with whom the person is in a relationship as a couple where one
-- or each of them provides personal or financial commitment and support of a domestic nature for the material benefit of
-- the other, irrespective of their genders and whether or not they are living under the same roof, but does not include
-- a person who provides domestic support and personal care to the person -- (i) for fee or reward; or (ii) on behalf of
-- another person or an organisation ..." (2)(b): all the circumstances are taken into account; (2)(c): not a domestic
-- partner "only because they are co-tenants" (implied in the couple question). A "means" definition.
@export Was I the "domestic partner" of the person who died (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 3)? A domestic partner ranks with a spouse as senior next of kin.
GIVEN `were we in a registered relationship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the person were in a relationship registered under the Relationships Act 2008
      `am I an adult` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are 18 or over
      `were we married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were married to the person (then you are their spouse, not their domestic partner)
      `were we a couple` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the person were in a relationship as a couple in which one or each of you gave personal or financial commitment and domestic support for the other's benefit, of any gender, living together or not (being co-tenants alone is not enough)
      `did I care for them for pay or for someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the person domestic support and personal care for a fee or reward, or on behalf of another person or an organisation (a government agency, company or charity)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Was I their domestic partner` MEANS
       `were we in a registered relationship`
    OR (`am I an adult` AND (NOT `were we married`) AND `were we a couple` AND (NOT `did I care for them for pay or for someone else`))

§§ `Was it a reportable or reviewable death`

GIVEN a1 IS A BOOLEAN, a2 IS A BOOLEAN, a3 IS A BOOLEAN, a4 IS A BOOLEAN, a5 IS A BOOLEAN, a6 IS A BOOLEAN, a7 IS A BOOLEAN,
      a8 IS A BOOLEAN, a9 IS A BOOLEAN, a10 IS A BOOLEAN, a11 IS A BOOLEAN, a12 IS A `Custody or care`, a13 IS A BOOLEAN,
      a14 IS A BOOLEAN, a15 IS A BOOLEAN, a16 IS A BOOLEAN, a17 IS A BOOLEAN, a18 IS A BOOLEAN, a19 IS A BOOLEAN,
      a20 IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Death facts`
`the death facts` a1 a2 a3 a4 a5 a6 a7 a8 a9 a10 a11 a12 a13 a14 a15 a16 a17 a18 a19 a20 MEANS
    `Death facts` WITH
        `the body is in Victoria`                                        IS a1
        `the death occurred in Victoria`                                 IS a2
        `the cause of the death occurred in Victoria`                    IS a3
        `the person ordinarily resided in Victoria`                      IS a4
        `it was a still-birth`                                           IS a5
        `it appears unexpected`                                          IS a6
        `it appears unnatural or violent`                                IS a7
        `it appears to result from an accident or injury`                IS a8
        `it occurred during a medical procedure`                         IS a9
        `it followed a medical procedure and is or may be related`       IS a10
        `a doctor would not have reasonably expected it`                 IS a11
        `the custody or care`                                            IS a12
        `the person was a mental health patient`                         IS a13
        `the person was under the Justice Secretary or a police officer` IS a14
        `the person was under a non-custodial supervision order`         IS a15
        `the identity is unknown`                                        IS a16
        `no s 37(1) notice is signed or likely`                          IS a17
        `the cause is not certified where it occurred, nor likely to be` IS a18
        `a prescribed class or circumstances`                            IS a19
        `voluntary assisted dying in accordance with the 2017 Act`       IS a20

@export Is the death a "reportable death" that must be reported to a coroner (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 4)? It needs a link with Victoria and a death of a listed kind: unexpected, unnatural, violent, from accident or injury, an unexpected death in or after a medical procedure, in custody or care, and others. A death by voluntary assisted dying under the 2017 Act is not reportable; a still-birth is not a death.
GIVEN `is the body in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is in Victoria
      `did the death occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in Victoria
      `did the cause of death occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cause of the death occurred in Victoria (for example, the accident or the injury)
      `did they ordinarily live in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person ordinarily lived in Victoria at the time of death
      `was it a still-birth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a still-birth within the meaning of the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1996
      `does the death appear unexpected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have been unexpected
      `does it appear unnatural or violent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have been unnatural or violent
      `did it result from an accident or injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have resulted, directly or indirectly, from an accident or injury
      `did it occur during a medical procedure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred during a medical procedure (one performed by or under the supervision of a doctor, including imaging, internal examination and surgery)
      `did it follow a medical procedure and may be related` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death followed a medical procedure and is or may be causally related to it
      `was the death not expected by a doctor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a registered medical practitioner would NOT, immediately before the procedure, have reasonably expected the death (answer yes if it was not expected)
      `were they in custody or care` IS A `Custody or care` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was in custody or care in one of the ways the Act lists, and which
      `were they a mental health patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was a patient within the meaning of the Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022
      `were they under the Justice Secretary or police` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was under the control, care or custody of the Secretary to the Department of Justice and Community Safety or a police officer
      `were they under a supervision order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was subject to a non-custodial supervision order under s 26 or 38ZH of the Crimes (Mental Impairment and Unfitness to be Tried) Act 1997
      `is their identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person who died is unknown
      `is no death notice signed or likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death in Victoria: whether the doctor's notice of the death under s 37(1) of the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1996 has not been signed and is not likely to be
      `is the cause uncertified where they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside Victoria: whether the cause of death has not been certified by a person authorised there, and is not likely to be
      `is it a prescribed class or circumstance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was of a class, or the death occurred in circumstances, prescribed by regulations (the Coroners Regulations are not deposited here)
      `was it voluntary assisted dying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was administered, or self-administered, a voluntary assisted dying substance in accordance with the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2017
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is the death a reportable death` MEANS
    `s 4 -- a reportable death` (`the death facts` `is the body in Victoria` `did the death occur in Victoria` `did the cause of death occur in Victoria` `did they ordinarily live in Victoria` `was it a still-birth` `does the death appear unexpected` `does it appear unnatural or violent` `did it result from an accident or injury` `did it occur during a medical procedure` `did it follow a medical procedure and may be related` `was the death not expected by a doctor` `were they in custody or care` `were they a mental health patient` `were they under the Justice Secretary or police` `were they under a supervision order` `is their identity unknown` `is no death notice signed or likely` `is the cause uncertified where they died` `is it a prescribed class or circumstance` `was it voluntary assisted dying`)

@export Is the death of a child a "reviewable death", reported to the State Coroner (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 5)? It is the death of a child who is the second or later child of a parent to die, with a link with Victoria, unless the child was born in and never left hospital, died in hospital, and the death is not reportable.
GIVEN `was the child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died was under 18
      `is the child the second or later of a parent to die` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child is the second or later child of one of their parents to have died
      `did the child die in hospital` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in a hospital (a public or private hospital, public health service or denominational hospital under the Health Services Act 1988)
      `had the child always been in hospital` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was born at a hospital and had always been an in-patient of a hospital (time spent only in transfer between hospitals does not count)
      `is the body in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is in Victoria
      `did the death occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in Victoria
      `did the cause of death occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cause of the death occurred in Victoria
      `did they ordinarily live in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child ordinarily lived in Victoria at the time of death
      `was it a still-birth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a still-birth
      `does the death appear unexpected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have been unexpected
      `does it appear unnatural or violent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have been unnatural or violent
      `did it result from an accident or injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have resulted, directly or indirectly, from an accident or injury
      `did it occur during a medical procedure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred during a medical procedure
      `did it follow a medical procedure and may be related` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death followed a medical procedure and is or may be causally related to it
      `was the death not expected by a doctor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a registered medical practitioner would NOT, immediately before the procedure, have reasonably expected the death
      `were they in custody or care` IS A `Custody or care` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the child was in custody or care in one of the ways the Act lists, and which
      `were they a mental health patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before death, the child was a patient within the meaning of the Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022
      `were they under the Justice Secretary or police` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was under the control, care or custody of the Justice Secretary or a police officer
      `were they under a supervision order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was subject to a non-custodial supervision order
      `is their identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the child is unknown
      `is no death notice signed or likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death in Victoria: whether the doctor's notice of death has not been signed and is not likely to be
      `is the cause uncertified where they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside Victoria: whether the cause of death has not been certified there, and is not likely to be
      `is it a prescribed class or circumstance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death is of a prescribed class or in prescribed circumstances
      `was it voluntary assisted dying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was voluntary assisted dying in accordance with the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2017
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is the death a reviewable death` MEANS
    `s 5 -- a reviewable death` (`the death facts` `is the body in Victoria` `did the death occur in Victoria` `did the cause of death occur in Victoria` `did they ordinarily live in Victoria` `was it a still-birth` `does the death appear unexpected` `does it appear unnatural or violent` `did it result from an accident or injury` `did it occur during a medical procedure` `did it follow a medical procedure and may be related` `was the death not expected by a doctor` `were they in custody or care` `were they a mental health patient` `were they under the Justice Secretary or police` `were they under a supervision order` `is their identity unknown` `is no death notice signed or likely` `is the cause uncertified where they died` `is it a prescribed class or circumstance` `was it voluntary assisted dying`) `was the child under 18` `is the child the second or later of a parent to die` `did the child die in hospital` `had the child always been in hospital`

§§ `Reporting the death`

-- s 12(1) "A person who has reasonable grounds to believe that a reportable death has not been reported must report it
-- without delay to a coroner, the Institute or the police officer in charge of a police station. 20 penalty units." The
-- "who"-clause read as a "where" condition: unmet, JUST FALSE (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05; fork F4).
@export Must I report the death to a coroner (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 12(1))? Anyone with reasonable grounds to believe a reportable death has not been reported must report it without delay to a coroner, the Victorian Institute of Forensic Medicine or the police officer in charge of a police station; failing to is an offence (20 penalty units).
GIVEN `do I believe it has not been reported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to believe that the death is a reportable death and that it has not been reported
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I report the death` MEANS
    IF `do I believe it has not been reported` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 12(2) "A member of the immediate family of a deceased person may report the death to the coroner or the Institute if
-- the person was a person discharged from a designated mental health service ... within 3 months immediately before the
-- person's death." The "if" unmet: JUST FALSE ("s 12(2) gives no permission on these facts"); not immediate family:
-- NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*. The window includes the day 3 months before the death (fork F3).
@export May I, as family, report the death to a coroner because they had been discharged from a mental health service (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 12(2))? A member of the immediate family may, if the person was discharged from a designated mental health service within 3 months immediately before death; a coroner may then investigate (s 14(2)).
GIVEN `what is my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse or domestic partner, son or daughter, parent, sibling, executor named in the will, personal representative, or none of these
      `has the coroner determined it is me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has determined you to be the senior next of kin (s 3(3))
      `were they discharged from a mental health service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person had been discharged from a designated mental health service within the meaning of the Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022
      `the day of discharge` IS A DATE @desc The day the person was discharged (any date if they were not)
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I report the death after a mental health discharge` MEANS
    IF NOT (`s 3 -- I am immediate family` `what is my relationship` `has the coroner determined it is me`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF     `were they discharged from a mental health service`
            AND (NOT (`the day of discharge` `is before` (`the date` 3 `months before` `the day of death`)))
            AND (NOT (`the day of discharge` `is after` `the day of death`))
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `The investigation`

DECLARE `Investigation answer` IS ONE OF
    `a coroner must investigate (s 15)`
    `a coroner may investigate (s 14)`
    `the investigation must be discontinued, over 100 years (s 16(5))`
    `not a reportable death on these facts (ss 14(3), 16)`

-- s 14(1) "A coroner may investigate a death that is or may be a reportable death if the death appears to have occurred
-- within 100 years before the death was reported to a coroner." (2) "A coroner may investigate a death reported to the
-- coroner under section 12(2)." s 15 "A coroner must investigate the death of a person if -- (a) it appears to the
-- coroner that the death, or the cause of death, occurred in Victoria; and (b) ... a reportable death; and (c) ... within
-- 50 years before the death was reported to a coroner; and (d) an interstate coroner has not investigated, is not
-- investigating, and does not intend to investigate, the death." s 16(5): "Whether or not a death is a reportable death,
-- the coroner must discontinue the investigation ... if the coroner determines that the death probably occurred more than
-- 100 years before it was reported". What "appears" to the coroner is taken from the facts as stated (fork F6).
@export Must a coroner investigate the death (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) ss 14 to 16)? A coroner must investigate a reportable death that occurred, or whose cause occurred, in Victoria within 50 years before it was reported, unless an interstate coroner deals with it; may investigate one within 100 years; and must stop if it probably occurred more than 100 years before. A child's reviewable death may also be investigated by the State Coroner (s 19).
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died, or probably died
      `the day it was reported` IS A DATE @desc The day the death was reported to a coroner
      `has an interstate coroner taken it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner of another State or Territory has investigated, is investigating, or intends to investigate the death
      `did family report it after a mental health discharge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was reported by a family member under s 12(2) (discharge from a designated mental health service within 3 months before death)
      `is the body in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is in Victoria
      `did the death occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in Victoria
      `did the cause of death occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cause of the death occurred in Victoria
      `did they ordinarily live in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person ordinarily lived in Victoria at the time of death
      `was it a still-birth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a still-birth
      `does the death appear unexpected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have been unexpected
      `does it appear unnatural or violent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have been unnatural or violent
      `did it result from an accident or injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have resulted, directly or indirectly, from an accident or injury
      `did it occur during a medical procedure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred during a medical procedure
      `did it follow a medical procedure and may be related` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death followed a medical procedure and is or may be causally related to it
      `was the death not expected by a doctor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a registered medical practitioner would NOT, immediately before the procedure, have reasonably expected the death
      `were they in custody or care` IS A `Custody or care` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was in custody or care in one of the ways the Act lists, and which
      `were they a mental health patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was a patient within the meaning of the Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022
      `were they under the Justice Secretary or police` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was under the control, care or custody of the Justice Secretary or a police officer
      `were they under a supervision order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was subject to a non-custodial supervision order
      `is their identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person is unknown
      `is no death notice signed or likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death in Victoria: whether the doctor's notice of death has not been signed and is not likely to be
      `is the cause uncertified where they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside Victoria: whether the cause of death has not been certified there, and is not likely to be
      `is it a prescribed class or circumstance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death is of a prescribed class or in prescribed circumstances
      `was it voluntary assisted dying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was voluntary assisted dying in accordance with the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2017
GIVETH AN `Investigation answer`
`Must a coroner investigate the death` MEANS
    IF `the day of death` `is before` (`the date` 1200 `months before` `the day it was reported`)
    THEN `the investigation must be discontinued, over 100 years (s 16(5))`
    ELSE IF     `reportable`
            AND (`did the death occur in Victoria` OR `did the cause of death occur in Victoria`)
            AND (NOT (`the day of death` `is before` (`the date` 600 `months before` `the day it was reported`)))
            AND (NOT `has an interstate coroner taken it`)
         THEN `a coroner must investigate (s 15)`
         ELSE IF `reportable` OR `did family report it after a mental health discharge`
              THEN `a coroner may investigate (s 14)`
              ELSE `not a reportable death on these facts (ss 14(3), 16)`
    WHERE
        `reportable` MEANS `s 4 -- a reportable death` (`the death facts` `is the body in Victoria` `did the death occur in Victoria` `did the cause of death occur in Victoria` `did they ordinarily live in Victoria` `was it a still-birth` `does the death appear unexpected` `does it appear unnatural or violent` `did it result from an accident or injury` `did it occur during a medical procedure` `did it follow a medical procedure and may be related` `was the death not expected by a doctor` `were they in custody or care` `were they a mental health patient` `were they under the Justice Secretary or police` `were they under a supervision order` `is their identity unknown` `is no death notice signed or likely` `is the cause uncertified where they died` `is it a prescribed class or circumstance` `was it voluntary assisted dying`)

DECLARE `Natural causes exit` IS ONE OF
    `the coroner need not continue (s 17) and may discontinue (s 17A)`
    `the coroner need not continue (s 17)`
    `the coroner may discontinue (s 17A)`
    `neither s 17 nor s 17A applies`

-- s 17(1) "A coroner is not required to continue an investigation into a reportable death if -- (a) ... not a death
-- referred to in section 4(2)(a) (other than a death that appears to have been unexpected); and (ab) ... not a death
-- referred to in section 4(2)(b), (c), (d), (e), (f), (g) or (j); and (b) a medical investigator conducts a medical
-- examination on the deceased person and provides a report to the coroner that includes an opinion that the death was due
-- to natural causes; and (d) the coroner determines that the death is not a reviewable death."
-- s 17A(1) "A coroner may discontinue an investigation into a reportable death if -- (a) ... a reportable death only
-- because -- (i) it is unexpected; or (ii) ... section 4(2)(h); or (iii) ... section 4(2)(i); and (b) a medical
-- investigator conducts a preliminary examination; and (c) a pathologist or a registered medical practitioner under the
-- general supervision of a pathologist -- (i) provides an opinion to the coroner that the death was due to natural causes;
-- and (ii) is willing to notify the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of the cause of death ...; and (d) the coroner
-- determines that the death is not a reviewable death." The coroner's determinations are taken from the facts as stated.
@export Can the coroner stop investigating because the death was from natural causes (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) ss 17, 17A)? Only for a death reportable just because it was unexpected or uncertified, with a medical opinion of natural causes, and not a child's reviewable death. After a s 17A discontinuance the senior next of kin may apply to re-open it (s 17B) and the coroner need make no findings (s 17A(5)).
GIVEN `did a medical investigator report natural causes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical investigator conducted a medical examination (a preliminary examination, identification procedure or autopsy) and reported to the coroner an opinion that the death was due to natural causes
      `was a preliminary examination done` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical investigator conducted a preliminary examination (examination without dissection: visual, samples, imaging, records)
      `did a pathologist give a natural causes opinion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a pathologist, or a doctor under a pathologist's supervision, gave the coroner an opinion that the death was due to natural causes
      `is the pathologist willing to notify the Registrar` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that pathologist or doctor is willing to notify the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of the cause of death
      `was the child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died was under 18
      `is the child the second or later of a parent to die` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a child: whether they are the second or later child of a parent to have died
      `did the child die in hospital` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a child: whether the death occurred in a hospital
      `had the child always been in hospital` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a child: whether they were born at a hospital and had always been an in-patient
      `is the body in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is in Victoria
      `did the death occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in Victoria
      `did the cause of death occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cause of the death occurred in Victoria
      `did they ordinarily live in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person ordinarily lived in Victoria at the time of death
      `was it a still-birth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a still-birth
      `does the death appear unexpected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have been unexpected
      `does it appear unnatural or violent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have been unnatural or violent
      `did it result from an accident or injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to have resulted, directly or indirectly, from an accident or injury
      `did it occur during a medical procedure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred during a medical procedure
      `did it follow a medical procedure and may be related` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death followed a medical procedure and is or may be causally related to it
      `was the death not expected by a doctor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a registered medical practitioner would NOT, immediately before the procedure, have reasonably expected the death
      `were they in custody or care` IS A `Custody or care` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was in custody or care in one of the ways the Act lists, and which
      `were they a mental health patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was a patient within the meaning of the Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022
      `were they under the Justice Secretary or police` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was under the control, care or custody of the Justice Secretary or a police officer
      `were they under a supervision order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was subject to a non-custodial supervision order
      `is their identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person is unknown
      `is no death notice signed or likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death in Victoria: whether the doctor's notice of death has not been signed and is not likely to be
      `is the cause uncertified where they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside Victoria: whether the cause of death has not been certified there, and is not likely to be
      `is it a prescribed class or circumstance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death is of a prescribed class or in prescribed circumstances
      `was it voluntary assisted dying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was voluntary assisted dying in accordance with the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2017
GIVETH A `Natural causes exit`
`Can the investigation stop on natural causes` MEANS
    IF `s 17` AND `s 17A`
    THEN `the coroner need not continue (s 17) and may discontinue (s 17A)`
    ELSE IF `s 17`
         THEN `the coroner need not continue (s 17)`
         ELSE IF `s 17A`
              THEN `the coroner may discontinue (s 17A)`
              ELSE `neither s 17 nor s 17A applies`
    WHERE
        `the facts` MEANS `the death facts` `is the body in Victoria` `did the death occur in Victoria` `did the cause of death occur in Victoria` `did they ordinarily live in Victoria` `was it a still-birth` `does the death appear unexpected` `does it appear unnatural or violent` `did it result from an accident or injury` `did it occur during a medical procedure` `did it follow a medical procedure and may be related` `was the death not expected by a doctor` `were they in custody or care` `were they a mental health patient` `were they under the Justice Secretary or police` `were they under a supervision order` `is their identity unknown` `is no death notice signed or likely` `is the cause uncertified where they died` `is it a prescribed class or circumstance` `was it voluntary assisted dying`
        `only because` MEANS `ss 17, 17A -- reportable only because unexpected, (h) or (i)` `the facts`
        `not reviewable` MEANS NOT (`s 5 -- a reviewable death` `the facts` `was the child under 18` `is the child the second or later of a parent to die` `did the child die in hospital` `had the child always been in hospital`)
        `s 17` MEANS `only because` AND `did a medical investigator report natural causes` AND `not reviewable`
        `s 17A` MEANS `only because` AND `was a preliminary examination done` AND `did a pathologist give a natural causes opinion` AND `is the pathologist willing to notify the Registrar` AND `not reviewable`

-- s 17B(1) "The Coroners Court, on its own motion, or on the application of a person specified in subsection (2), may
-- order that an investigation discontinued under section 17A be re-opened if the Court is satisfied there are new facts
-- and circumstances ..."; (2) "(a) the senior next of kin; (b) the Chief Commissioner of Police; (c) any other person --
-- (i) who the Court is satisfied has a sufficient interest ...; and (ii) if the Court is satisfied it is appropriate to
-- give the person leave"; (4) "A person must not apply ... if an application based on the same, or substantially the
-- same, grounds or evidence had been made and the Coroners Court refused to make an order". No time limit (finding 6).
@export Can I apply to the Coroners Court to re-open an investigation that was discontinued on natural causes (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 17B)? The senior next of kin may; anyone else needs the Court's leave and a sufficient interest. Not again on the same grounds once refused. The Court re-opens only if satisfied there are new facts and circumstances. No time limit is set; a refusal can be appealed within 90 days (s 84A).
GIVEN `was it discontinued under s 17A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner discontinued the investigation under s 17A (natural causes, with a direction to notify the Registrar)
      `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `has the court given me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are not the senior next of kin: whether the Court is satisfied you have a sufficient interest in re-opening it and that it is appropriate to give you leave to apply
      `was the same application refused before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application on the same, or substantially the same, grounds or evidence was made before and the Court refused to re-open
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to re-open the discontinued investigation` MEANS
    IF NOT `was it discontinued under s 17A`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `was the same application refused before`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `am I the senior next of kin` OR `has the court given me leave`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE NOTHING

§§ `The autopsy`

-- s 26(4) "A direction under section 25(2) does not take effect until -- (a) subject to paragraphs (b) and (c) 48 hours
-- after the required notice has been given under subsection (1); or (b) subject to paragraph (c), if -- (i) a request has
-- been made under subsection (2)(a), 48 hours after the notice is given under subsection (3); or (ii) the remainder of the
-- period ... has been waived by the senior next of kin under subsection (2)(b), on the giving of the waiver; or (c) a
-- direction is given under subsection (5)." An "until" bar (ruling (d) of 2026-11-05): JUST TRUE while it holds, JUST
-- FALSE after; never a claim that the autopsy has been done. Hours from the notice being given.
GIVEN `immediate autopsy directed`       IS A BOOLEAN
      `earlier autopsy directed`         IS A BOOLEAN
      `I waived the rest of the period`  IS A BOOLEAN
      `notice of the direction given`    IS A BOOLEAN
      `hours since that notice`          IS A NUMBER
      `I asked for reconsideration`      IS A BOOLEAN
      `notice of confirmation given`     IS A BOOLEAN
      `hours since the confirmation`     IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 26(4) -- the autopsy direction has not yet taken effect` `immediate autopsy directed` `earlier autopsy directed` `I waived the rest of the period` `notice of the direction given` `hours since that notice` `I asked for reconsideration` `notice of confirmation given` `hours since the confirmation` IF
    IF `immediate autopsy directed` OR `earlier autopsy directed` OR `I waived the rest of the period`
    THEN FALSE
    ELSE IF `I asked for reconsideration`
         THEN NOT (`notice of confirmation given` AND `hours since the confirmation` AT LEAST 48)
         ELSE NOT (`notice of the direction given` AND `hours since that notice` AT LEAST 48)

@export Can I still ask the coroner to reconsider an autopsy, or waive the waiting time (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 26(2))? The senior next of kin may, within 48 hours after receiving notice of the coroner's direction, ask the coroner to reconsider it, or consent to the autopsy going ahead without delay.
GIVEN `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `hours since I received the notice` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since you received the coroner's notice that an autopsy was directed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I ask the coroner to reconsider the autopsy` MEANS
    IF NOT `am I the senior next of kin`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `hours since I received the notice` AT MOST 48 THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

@export Is the coroner's autopsy direction still held back, so the autopsy cannot yet be performed (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 26(4), (5))? It takes effect 48 hours after the senior next of kin is notified; if they ask for reconsideration and the coroner confirms it, 48 hours after notice of that; at once if they waive the wait; and at once if the coroner directs an immediate or earlier autopsy.
GIVEN `did the coroner direct an immediate autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner directed that the autopsy be performed immediately, without notice (s 26(5)(a): appropriate in the circumstances, or no senior next of kin can be found)
      `did the coroner direct an earlier autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner directed it be performed less than 48 hours after notice of confirmation because the senior next of kin said they will not appeal (s 26(5)(b))
      `did I waive the rest of the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the senior next of kin consented, under the rules, to waiving the rest of the 48 hours
      `was notice of the direction given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has given the senior next of kin notice of the direction
      `hours since notice of the direction` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since that notice was given
      `did I ask for reconsideration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the senior next of kin asked the coroner to reconsider the direction
      `was notice of confirmation given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after reconsidering, the coroner gave the senior next of kin written notice that the autopsy is necessary and the direction was appropriate (s 26(3))
      `hours since notice of confirmation` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since that notice of confirmation was given
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is the autopsy direction still held back` MEANS
    `s 26(4) -- the autopsy direction has not yet taken effect` `did the coroner direct an immediate autopsy` `did the coroner direct an earlier autopsy` `did I waive the rest of the period` `was notice of the direction given` `hours since notice of the direction` `did I ask for reconsideration` `was notice of confirmation given` `hours since notice of confirmation`

-- s 79(1) "If a coroner gives a direction ... that an autopsy be performed, the senior next of kin may appeal against
-- that direction and any conditions ..."; (2) "An appeal under subsection (1) must be made before the direction takes
-- effect." The period fixed in the text: after it, JUST FALSE. Not the senior next of kin: NOTHING-kind *limited by its
-- own wording*.
@export Can I still appeal to the Supreme Court against the coroner's autopsy direction (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 79(1), (2))? The senior next of kin may, but only before the direction takes effect (see the s 26(4) question). No extension of time is available (s 86).
GIVEN `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `did the coroner direct an immediate autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner directed an immediate autopsy (s 26(5)(a))
      `did the coroner direct an earlier autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner directed an earlier autopsy after you said you will not appeal (s 26(5)(b))
      `did I waive the rest of the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you waived the rest of the 48 hours
      `was notice of the direction given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given notice of the direction
      `hours since notice of the direction` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since that notice was given
      `did I ask for reconsideration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the coroner to reconsider
      `was notice of confirmation given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner gave you written notice confirming the direction (s 26(3))
      `hours since notice of confirmation` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since that notice of confirmation was given
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I still appeal the autopsy direction` MEANS
    IF NOT `am I the senior next of kin`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `s 26(4) -- the autopsy direction has not yet taken effect` `did the coroner direct an immediate autopsy` `did the coroner direct an earlier autopsy` `did I waive the rest of the period` `was notice of the direction given` `hours since notice of the direction` `did I ask for reconsideration` `was notice of confirmation given` `hours since notice of confirmation`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 27(1) "If a coroner has control of a body, any person may ask the coroner to direct that an autopsy be performed";
-- (2) on refusal, written reasons without delay. s 79(3) "If a coroner refuses a request under section 27 ... (a) the
-- senior next of kin; or (b) the person who requested the autopsy ... may appeal"; (4) "An appeal under subsection (3)
-- must be made within 48 hours after the person receives notice of the refusal ...".
@export Can I still appeal to the Supreme Court against the coroner's refusal to direct an autopsy (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) ss 27, 79(3), (4))? Anyone may ask for an autopsy of a body the coroner controls; if refused, the senior next of kin or the person who asked may appeal within 48 hours after receiving notice of the refusal. No extension is available (s 86).
GIVEN `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `did I ask for the autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the coroner to direct the autopsy
      `hours since I received the refusal` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since you received notice of the refusal (its written reasons)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal the refusal of an autopsy in time` MEANS
    IF NOT (`am I the senior next of kin` OR `did I ask for the autopsy`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `hours since I received the refusal` AT MOST 48 THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Exhumation`

-- s 46(5) "An authorisation under this section does not take effect until 48 hours after the senior next of kin is
-- notified of the authorisation under subsection (3) or any further period specified by the State Coroner unless -- (a)
-- the State Coroner directs that the exhumation be conducted immediately; or (b) within that period, the senior next of
-- kin advises the State Coroner that he or she will not appeal". "Further period" is read as added to the 48 hours (fork
-- F7). An "until" bar: JUST TRUE while it holds.
@export Is the State Coroner's authorisation to exhume the body still held back (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 46(5))? It takes effect 48 hours (plus any further period the State Coroner specifies) after the senior next of kin is notified, unless the State Coroner directs it be done immediately or the senior next of kin says they will not appeal.
GIVEN `was an immediate exhumation directed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the State Coroner directed that the exhumation be conducted immediately
      `did the senior next of kin waive an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, within the period, the senior next of kin told the State Coroner they will not appeal
      `hours since the senior next of kin was notified` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since the senior next of kin was notified of the authorisation
      `further hours specified` IS A NUMBER @desc Any further period the State Coroner specified, in hours (0 if none)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is the exhumation still held back` MEANS
        (NOT `was an immediate exhumation directed`)
    AND (NOT `did the senior next of kin waive an appeal`)
    AND `hours since the senior next of kin was notified` LESS THAN (48 PLUS `further hours specified`)

-- s 81(1) "If the State Coroner notifies a person under section 46(3) of the authorisation of an exhumation, the senior
-- next of kin and any other person who received notice under section 46(3) may appeal ..."; (2) "within -- (a) 48 hours;
-- or (b) any further period specified by the State Coroner under section 46(5) -- after the person receives notice".
@export Can I still appeal to the Supreme Court against an authorisation to exhume the body (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 81(1), (2))? The senior next of kin, or anyone else notified (a cemetery trust, a landowner), may appeal within 48 hours, plus any further period the State Coroner specified, after receiving notice. No extension is available (s 86).
GIVEN `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `was I notified of the authorisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the State Coroner gave you notice of the authorisation under s 46(3)
      `hours since I received the notice` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since you received notice of the authorisation
      `further hours specified` IS A NUMBER @desc Any further period the State Coroner specified, in hours (0 if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I still appeal the exhumation authorisation` MEANS
    IF NOT (`am I the senior next of kin` OR `was I notified of the authorisation`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `hours since I received the notice` AT MOST (48 PLUS `further hours specified`) THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Release of the body`

-- s 48(2) "If 2 or more applicants apply for release of the body, the coroner must determine the person to whom the body
-- is to be released on the basis of who has the better claim." (3) the principles: "(a) if the person named in the will as
-- an executor is an applicant, the body of the deceased should be released to the executor; (b) if a person specified
-- under paragraph (a) is not an applicant, the body should be released to the senior next of kin; (c) if there appear to
-- be 2 or more applicants who are the senior next of kin ..., the coroner should determine ... having regard to any
-- principles of common law ...; (d) if no person referred to in paragraph (a) or (b) is an applicant, ... common law".
-- JUST TRUE: the principles point to me; JUST FALSE: they point to someone else; NOTHING-kind *points outside the
-- sources* (common law) or *limited by its own wording* (one applicant: s 48(2) does not apply; not an applicant).
@export Do the Act's principles point to the body being released to me, where more than one person has applied (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 48)? First the executor named in the will, if an applicant; then the senior next of kin; where two or more senior next of kin apply, or neither applies, the common law decides. The coroner must have regard to these principles. An order can be appealed within 48 hours (s 85).
GIVEN `did I apply for the body` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied to the coroner for the body to be released to you
      `how many applied` IS A NUMBER @desc How many people applied for the body, you included
      `am I the executor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person named in the will as an executor
      `did the executor apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person named in the will as an executor is one of the applicants
      `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a senior next of kin
      `how many senior next of kin applied` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the applicants are senior next of kin (for example two adult children), you included
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do the principles point to releasing the body to me` MEANS
    IF (NOT `did I apply for the body`) OR `how many applied` LESS THAN 2
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `did the executor apply`
         THEN IF `am I the executor` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `how many senior next of kin applied` AT LEAST 2
              THEN NOTHING
              ELSE IF `how many senior next of kin applied` EQUALS 1
                   THEN IF `am I the senior next of kin` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
                   ELSE NOTHING

-- s 85(1) "A person who applied to have a body released to him or her under section 48 may appeal against an order to
-- release the body or the terms of that release ..."; (2) "within 48 hours after the determination of the coroner is
-- made".
@export Can I still appeal to the Supreme Court against the coroner's order releasing the body, or its terms (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 85)? A person who applied for the body may appeal within 48 hours after the coroner's determination. No extension is available (s 86).
GIVEN `did I apply for the body` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied to the coroner for the body to be released to you
      `hours since the determination` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since the coroner made the determination
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal the release order in time` MEANS
    IF NOT `did I apply for the body`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `hours since the determination` AT MOST 48 THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `When the coroner asks you for information`

@export Have I given the coroner the document or statement they required in time (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 42)? A person served with a coroner's requirement to give a document, or to prepare a statement, must comply within the period the requirement specifies; failing to without a lawful excuse is an offence (20 penalty units).
GIVEN `the day I was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the requirement was served on you
      `the last day allowed` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period the requirement gives you to comply
      `have I complied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the coroner the document or statement
      `the day I complied` IS A DATE @desc The day you did so (any date if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What became of the duty`
`Have I met the coroner's requirement in time` MEANS
    `s 42(3) -- what became of my compliance` `the day I was served` `the last day allowed` `have I complied` `the day I complied` `today`

DECLARE `What I am required to give` IS ONE OF
    `a document`
    `information, a statement or other assistance`

-- s 50(1) "It is a reasonable excuse for a natural person to refuse or fail to give information or do any other thing that
-- the person is required to do by or under section 32, 33, 34 or 42, if the giving of the information or the doing of
-- that other thing would tend to incriminate the person." (2) "Despite subsection (1), it is not a reasonable excuse for a
-- natural person to refuse or fail to produce a document ..." Not incriminating: NOTHING-kind *silent* (s 50 does not
-- speak; other excuses may exist). Not required under those sections: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Is it an excuse for me not to give the coroner what they asked for, because it would incriminate me (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 50)? Yes for information, a statement or other help under ss 32, 33, 34 or 42; no for producing a document.
GIVEN `was I required under ss 32, 33, 34 or 42` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were required to give the coroner information, assistance, a document or a statement as the person who reported the death (s 32), a doctor (s 33), a person who asked for a fire investigation (s 34), or under a coroner's requirement (s 42)
      `what am I required to give` IS A `What I am required to give` @desc A document, or information, a statement or other assistance
      `would it tend to incriminate me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether giving it would tend to show that you committed an offence
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is self-incrimination an excuse for me` MEANS
    IF NOT (`was I required under ss 32, 33, 34 or 42` AND `would it tend to incriminate me`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `what am I required to give` EQUALS `a document` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `The inquest`

-- s 52(2) "Subject to subsections (3) and (3A), a coroner must hold an inquest into a death if the death or cause of
-- death occurred in Victoria and -- (a) the coroner suspects the death was the result of homicide; or (b) the deceased
-- was, immediately before death, a person placed in custody or care; or (c) the identity of the deceased is unknown; or
-- (d) the death occurred in prescribed circumstances." (3) "The coroner is not required to hold an inquest in the
-- circumstances set out in subsection (2) if -- (a) the coroner believes the death probably occurred more than 50 years
-- before the death was reported ...; or (b) a person has been charged with an indictable offence in respect of the death
-- ...; or (c) an interstate coroner has investigated, is investigating, or intends to investigate, the death; or (d) the
-- death occurred outside Australia." (3A) "... not required ... in the circumstances set out in subsection (2)(b) if the
-- coroner considers that the death was due to natural causes."
@export Must the coroner hold an inquest (a public hearing) into the death (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 52(2) to (3A))? Yes where the death or its cause occurred in Victoria and homicide is suspected, the person was in custody or care (unless the coroner considers the death natural), the identity is unknown, or prescribed circumstances apply; not where it probably occurred over 50 years before it was reported, someone is charged with an indictable offence over it, an interstate coroner deals with it, or it occurred outside Australia. The coroner may hold one in any other case (s 52(1), (4)).
GIVEN `did the death or its cause occur in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death, or the cause of the death, occurred in Victoria
      `does the coroner suspect homicide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner suspects the death was the result of homicide
      `were they in custody or care` IS A `Custody or care` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was in custody or care in one of the ways the Act lists, and which
      `is their identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person is unknown
      `was it in prescribed circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in circumstances prescribed by regulations for s 52(2)(d) (not deposited here)
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died, or probably died
      `the day it was reported` IS A DATE @desc The day the death was reported to the coroner
      `has someone been charged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person has been charged with an indictable offence in respect of the death
      `has an interstate coroner taken it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner of another State or Territory has investigated, is investigating or intends to investigate the death
      `did the death occur outside Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred outside Australia
      `does the coroner consider it natural` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner considers the death was due to natural causes (for example on a medical investigator's report, s 52(3B))
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the coroner hold an inquest` MEANS
    IF NOT `did the death or its cause occur in Victoria`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF NOT `a head of s 52(2) applies`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `an exception of s 52(3) applies`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE JUST TRUE
    WHERE
        `a head of s 52(2) applies` MEANS
               `does the coroner suspect homicide`
            OR (`s 3 -- a person placed in custody or care` `were they in custody or care` AND (NOT `does the coroner consider it natural`))
            OR `is their identity unknown`
            OR `was it in prescribed circumstances`
        `an exception of s 52(3) applies` MEANS
               `the day of death` `is before` (`the date` 600 `months before` `the day it was reported`)
            OR `has someone been charged`
            OR `has an interstate coroner taken it`
            OR `did the death occur outside Australia`

@export Has the coroner answered my request for an inquest in time (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 52(5), (6), s 53(2), (3))? Anyone may ask the coroner to hold an inquest into a death or fire being investigated; within 3 months of receiving the request the coroner must tell the person in writing that an inquest will be held, will not be held, or is not yet decided. A decision not to hold one can be appealed within 3 months (s 82).
GIVEN `the day the coroner received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the coroner received your request for an inquest
      `has the coroner answered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has advised you in writing where the decision stands
      `the day the coroner answered` IS A DATE @desc The day the coroner did so (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What became of the duty`
`Has the coroner answered my inquest request in time` MEANS
    `s 52(6) -- what became of the coroner's answer` `the day the coroner received my request` `has the coroner answered` `the day the coroner answered` `today`

-- s 56 "A coroner may give a person leave to appear as an interested party at an inquest if the coroner is satisfied that
-- -- (a) the person has a sufficient interest in the inquest; and (b) it is appropriate for the person to be an interested
-- party." The "if" unmet: JUST FALSE.
@export Can I take part in the inquest as an "interested party" (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 56)? The coroner may give leave if satisfied you have a sufficient interest and it is appropriate. An interested party may appear, be represented, call for witnesses, question witnesses and make submissions (s 66), receives the coronial brief (s 115), and may appeal the findings (s 83(2)).
GIVEN `is the coroner satisfied I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied you have a sufficient interest in the inquest
      `is the coroner satisfied it is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied it is appropriate for you to be an interested party
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be an interested party` MEANS
    IF `is the coroner satisfied I have a sufficient interest` AND `is the coroner satisfied it is appropriate` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 66(3) "An interested party may appear or be represented by an Australian lawyer or, with the permission of the
-- coroner, by any other person, and may examine or cross-examine witnesses and make submissions." Without permission, a
-- non-lawyer: JUST FALSE (the condition "with the permission" read as "if"; fork F8). Not an interested party:
-- NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export May this person represent me at the inquest (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 66(3))? An interested party may appear personally, be represented by an Australian lawyer, or by anyone else with the coroner's permission.
GIVEN `am I an interested party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has given you leave to appear as an interested party (s 56)
      `is my representative a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who would represent you is an Australian lawyer
      `has the coroner permitted them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If not a lawyer: whether the coroner has given permission for that person to represent you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May this person represent me at the inquest` MEANS
    IF NOT `am I an interested party`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `is my representative a lawyer` OR `has the coroner permitted them` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 115(1) "Unless otherwise ordered by the coroner, the principal registrar must -- (a) provide the senior next of kin
-- ... written notice ... specifying -- (i) that reports have been given to a coroner as a result of a medical examination
-- ...; (ii) that the senior next of kin may request copies of those reports; and (iii) the manner ...; and (b) if an inquest
-- is to be held, provide an interested party with a copy of the coronial brief."
@export Must I be told about the medical examination reports and how to ask for copies (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 115(1)(a))? Unless the coroner orders otherwise, the senior next of kin must be given written notice that reports of a medical examination (including an autopsy) were given to the coroner, that copies may be requested, and how.
GIVEN `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `were reports given to the coroner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether reports of a medical examination of the person (preliminary examination, identification procedure or autopsy) have been given to the coroner
      `did the coroner order otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has ordered that this notice not be given
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be told about the medical reports` MEANS
    IF NOT `am I the senior next of kin`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `did the coroner order otherwise`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `were reports given to the coroner` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

@export Must I be given a copy of the coronial brief (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 115(1)(b), (7))? Unless the coroner orders otherwise, an interested party must be given a copy if an inquest is to be held. The brief holds, if available, the identification statement, medical examination reports, and the reports, statements and other material the coroner believes relevant.
GIVEN `am I an interested party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has given you leave to appear as an interested party (s 56)
      `is an inquest to be held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inquest is to be held
      `did the coroner order otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has ordered that the brief not be provided
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be given the coronial brief` MEANS
    IF NOT `am I an interested party`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `did the coroner order otherwise` OR (NOT `is an inquest to be held`) THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `Giving evidence at the inquest`

-- s 57(1) "This section applies if a witness objects to giving evidence ... on the ground that the evidence may tend to
-- prove that the witness -- (a) has committed an offence ...; or (b) is liable to a civil penalty ..."; (2) the coroner
-- determines whether there are reasonable grounds; (4) "The coroner may require the witness to give evidence if the
-- coroner is satisfied that -- (a) the evidence does not tend to prove that the witness has committed an offence against
-- or arising under, or is liable to a civil penalty under, a law of a foreign country; and (b) the interests of justice
-- require that the witness give the evidence." No reasonable grounds: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (s 57(4)
-- does not speak; the general power to order a witness to answer, s 55(2)(c), is not encoded).
@export Can the coroner make me give evidence I objected to because it may incriminate me (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 57(4))? Where the coroner finds reasonable grounds for the objection, only if the evidence does not tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty and the interests of justice require it; you then get a certificate protecting the evidence (s 57(5)).
GIVEN `did I object on that ground` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you objected to giving the evidence because it may tend to prove you committed an offence or are liable to a civil penalty
      `did the coroner find reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner determined there are reasonable grounds for your objection
      `would it tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence tends to prove an offence or civil penalty under the law of a foreign country
      `does justice require it, the coroner is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied the interests of justice require that you give the evidence
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the coroner make me give the evidence` MEANS
    IF NOT (`did I object on that ground` AND `did the coroner find reasonable grounds`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF (NOT `would it tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty`) AND `does justice require it, the coroner is satisfied`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 57(5) "If the witness either willingly gives the evidence without being required to do so under subsection (4), or
-- gives it after being required to do so ..., the coroner must cause the witness to be given a certificate"; (6) "...
-- also ... if -- (a) the objection has been overruled; and (b) after the evidence has been given, the coroner finds that
-- there were reasonable grounds for the objection." No objection: s 57 does not apply ("applies if"): JUST FALSE.
@export Must I be given a certificate protecting the evidence I gave at the inquest (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 57(5), (6))? After objecting on self-incrimination grounds, yes if the coroner found reasonable grounds and you gave the evidence (willingly or when required), or if the objection was overruled and the coroner later finds there were reasonable grounds.
GIVEN `did I object on that ground` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you objected to giving the evidence because it may tend to prove you committed an offence or are liable to a civil penalty
      `did the coroner find reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner determined, before you gave it, that there were reasonable grounds for the objection
      `did I give the evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the evidence
      `did the coroner later find reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, the objection having been overruled, the coroner found after the evidence was given that there were reasonable grounds for it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be given a certificate for my evidence` MEANS
    IF NOT `did I object on that ground`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `did I give the evidence` AND (`did the coroner find reasonable grounds` OR `did the coroner later find reasonable grounds`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 57(7) "In any proceeding in a court or before any person or body authorised by a law of this State, or by consent of
-- parties, to hear, receive and examine evidence -- (a) evidence given by a person in respect of which a certificate under
-- this section has been given; and (b) any information, document or thing obtained as a direct or indirect consequence of
-- the person having given evidence -- cannot be used against the person. However, this does not apply to a criminal
-- proceeding in respect of the falsity of the evidence." (8): despite any challenge to the certificate.
@export Is the evidence I gave under a s 57 certificate protected from use against me in another proceeding, such as a civil claim or prosecution (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 57(7), (8))? It and anything obtained from it cannot be used against you in a court or before a body authorised by Victorian law or by consent, except in a prosecution for the falsity of the evidence.
GIVEN `was I given a certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given a certificate under s 57 for the evidence
      `is it a court or authorised body` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other proceeding is in a court, or before a person or body authorised by Victorian law, or by consent of the parties, to hear evidence
      `is it a prosecution for falsity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other proceeding is a criminal proceeding about the falsity of the evidence you gave
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my certified evidence protected` MEANS
    IF NOT (`was I given a certificate` AND `is it a court or authorised body`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `is it a prosecution for falsity` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `Findings`

-- s 67(1) "A coroner investigating a death must find, if possible -- (a) the identity of the deceased; and (b) the cause of
-- death; and (c) unless subsection (2) applies, the circumstances in which the death occurred; and (d) any other
-- prescribed particulars." (2) "... need not make a finding with respect to the circumstances ... if -- (a) an inquest into
-- the death was not held; and (b) the coroner finds that -- (i) the deceased was not, immediately before the person died, a
-- person placed in custody or care; and (ii) there is no public interest to be served ..." s 71: not required to make any
-- s 67 finding if the coroner decided not to hold, or to discontinue, an inquest because a person has been charged with an
-- indictable offence, and considers the findings would be inappropriate. s 17A(5): not required where the coroner
-- directed notification of the Registrar under s 17A(2).
@export Must the coroner make findings on who died and the cause of death (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 67(1)(a), (b))? Yes, if possible, unless the investigation was discontinued on natural causes with a direction to notify the Registrar (s 17A(5)), or no inquest was held (or it was discontinued) because someone is charged with an indictable offence and the coroner considers findings inappropriate (s 71).
GIVEN `was it discontinued under s 17A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner discontinued the investigation under s 17A and directed a pathologist or doctor to notify the Registrar
      `was the inquest dropped for a charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner decided not to hold an inquest, or to discontinue it, because a person has been charged with an indictable offence in respect of the death
      `does the coroner consider findings inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of that decision, the coroner considers making the findings would be inappropriate
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the coroner find identity and cause` MEANS
    IF `was it discontinued under s 17A` OR (`was the inquest dropped for a charge` AND `does the coroner consider findings inappropriate`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

@export Must the coroner make a finding on the circumstances in which the death occurred (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 67(1)(c), (2), s 71, s 17A(5))? Yes, if possible, unless no inquest was held, the person was not in custody or care, and the coroner finds no public interest in it; or ss 71 or 17A(5) apply.
GIVEN `was an inquest held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inquest into the death was held
      `were they in custody or care` IS A `Custody or care` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was in custody or care in one of the ways the Act lists, and which
      `does the coroner find no public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner finds there is no public interest to be served in a finding on the circumstances
      `was it discontinued under s 17A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner discontinued the investigation under s 17A and directed notification of the Registrar
      `was the inquest dropped for a charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner decided not to hold, or to discontinue, an inquest because a person has been charged with an indictable offence in respect of the death
      `does the coroner consider findings inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of that decision, the coroner considers making the findings would be inappropriate
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the coroner find the circumstances of the death` MEANS
    IF `was it discontinued under s 17A` OR (`was the inquest dropped for a charge` AND `does the coroner consider findings inappropriate`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF (NOT `was an inquest held`) AND (NOT `s 3 -- a person placed in custody or care` `were they in custody or care`) AND `does the coroner find no public interest`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 69(1) "A coroner must not include in a finding or comment any statement that a person is, or may be, guilty of an
-- offence." (2) "Subsection (1) does not apply to prevent the inclusion in a comment of a statement relating to a
-- notification to the Director of Public Prosecutions under section 49."
@export Is the coroner forbidden to say in the findings or comments that someone is, or may be, guilty of an offence (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 69)? Yes, except that a comment may refer to the coroner's notification of the Director of Public Prosecutions (s 49).
GIVEN `is it in a comment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement would be in a comment, not in a finding
      `does it relate to a notice to the DPP` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement relates to the notification of the Director of Public Prosecutions that an indictable offence may have been committed (s 49(1))
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the coroner forbidden to say someone may be guilty` MEANS
    IF `is it in a comment` AND `does it relate to a notice to the DPP` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

DECLARE `What was offered` IS ONE OF
    `an expression of sorrow, regret or sympathy without a clear acknowledgement of fault`
    `an expression of sorrow that clearly acknowledges fault`
    `a reduction or waiver of fees for a service provided to the person who died`

-- s 70(1) "apology means an expression of sorrow, regret or sympathy but does not include a clear acknowledgement of
-- fault." (2) "In an investigation of a death or fire -- (a) an apology; or (b) a reduction or waiver of fees payable for
-- a service provided to the person who died -- does not constitute an admission as to any matter, for the purposes of
-- findings that are made under section 67 or 68." (3): oral or written, before or after the investigation began. (4):
-- admissibility of a statement of fact is not affected. A statement that clearly acknowledges fault is outside s 70:
-- NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Does an apology, or a hospital or provider reducing or waiving its fees, count as an admission for the coroner's findings (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 70)? No, for an expression of sorrow, regret or sympathy that does not clearly acknowledge fault, or a fee reduction or waiver, oral or written, before or after the investigation began. It speaks only to the coroner's findings, not to a civil claim.
GIVEN `what was offered` IS A `What was offered` @desc What was said or done: an expression of sorrow, regret or sympathy without a clear acknowledgement of fault; one that clearly acknowledges fault; or a reduction or waiver of fees for a service provided to the person who died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it an admission for the findings` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what was offered`
    WHEN `an expression of sorrow that clearly acknowledges fault` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `Publication` IS ONE OF
    `must be published on the internet`
    `may be published on the internet`
    `need not be published: the coroner ordered otherwise`

-- s 73(1) "Unless otherwise ordered by a coroner, the findings, comments and recommendations made following an inquest
-- must be published on the Internet in accordance with the rules." (1A) "Subject to subsection (1B), the findings,
-- comments and recommendations made following an investigation may be published ..." (1B) "A finding made following an
-- investigation of a death of a deceased who was, immediately before the death, a person placed in custody or care that
-- the death was due to natural causes must be published ..." (1B) has no "unless otherwise ordered" (finding 8).
@export Will the coroner's findings be published on the internet (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 73)? After an inquest they must be, unless the coroner orders otherwise; after an investigation without an inquest they may be; a finding that a person in custody or care died of natural causes must always be.
GIVEN `was an inquest held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the findings follow an inquest
      `did the coroner order otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner ordered that the findings not be published
      `were they in custody or care` IS A `Custody or care` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was in custody or care in one of the ways the Act lists, and which
      `was the finding natural causes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the finding is that the death was due to natural causes
GIVETH A `Publication`
`Will the findings be published` MEANS
    IF `s 3 -- a person placed in custody or care` `were they in custody or care` AND `was the finding natural causes`
    THEN `must be published on the internet`
    ELSE IF `was an inquest held`
         THEN IF `did the coroner order otherwise`
              THEN `need not be published: the coroner ordered otherwise`
              ELSE `must be published on the internet`
         ELSE `may be published on the internet`

-- s 74(1) "Subject to subsection (2), every person appearing before the Coroners Court who is represented by an Australian
-- lawyer is to bear their own costs in relation to the inquest." (2) "If ... a coroner is of the opinion that a person (the
-- first person) has acted unreasonably during an investigation or inquest, the coroner may order the first person to pay
-- all, or a specified part, of the expenses (other than economic loss) reasonably incurred by another person (the second
-- person) -- (a) as a result of the unreasonable actions of the first person; and (b) that relate to the participation of
-- the second person in the investigation or inquest." (3): recoverable as a debt.
@export What is the most the coroner can order someone who acted unreasonably to pay me for my expenses (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 74)? Otherwise each represented person bears their own costs. If the coroner is of the opinion the other person acted unreasonably, all or part of the expenses you reasonably incurred because of it, in taking part in the investigation or inquest, other than economic loss; recoverable as a debt. In dollars; 0 if the coroner is not of that opinion.
GIVEN `does the coroner think they acted unreasonably` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is of the opinion that the other person acted unreasonably during the investigation or inquest
      `my expenses from it` IS A NUMBER @desc The expenses, in dollars, you reasonably incurred as a result of their unreasonable actions and that relate to your taking part in the investigation or inquest
      `of which economic loss` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of those expenses is economic loss (for example lost wages), in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The most I can be awarded for unreasonable conduct` MEANS
    IF `does the coroner think they acted unreasonably`
    THEN max 0 (`my expenses from it` MINUS `of which economic loss`)
    ELSE 0

§§ `Changing or setting aside the findings`

-- s 76A(1) "The following persons may apply to the Coroners Court for the making of an amendment to the wording of a
-- decision ... -- (a) the senior next of kin ...; (b) an interested party in relation to an inquest in respect of the
-- death; (c) any other person if the Coroners Court is satisfied that -- (i) the person has a sufficient interest ...;
-- and (ii) it is appropriate for the person to be granted leave ..." (3) "An application under subsection (1) must be
-- made within 3 months after the date on which the decision was made." The list is not closed with "only": a person
-- outside it gets NOTHING-kind *list not closed*; late: JUST FALSE (the period fixed in the text).
@export Can I still apply to the Coroners Court to amend the wording of the coroner's decision (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 76A)? The senior next of kin, an interested party at the inquest, or another person with the Court's leave, within 3 months after the decision. The Court may amend it if consistent with the s 67(1) findings, not needing a re-opening, not prejudicial to another interested party, and otherwise appropriate.
GIVEN `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `was I an interested party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were an interested party at an inquest into the death
      `has the court given me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Otherwise: whether the Court is satisfied you have a sufficient interest in the investigation and it is appropriate to grant you leave
      `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the coroner's decision was made
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply, or would apply
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to amend the wording of the decision` MEANS
    IF NOT (`am I the senior next of kin` OR `was I an interested party` OR `has the court given me leave`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the day I apply` `is after` (`the date` 3 `months after` `the day of the decision`) THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 77(1), (1A): the Coroners Court may set aside findings on the application of "(a) the senior next of kin; (b) the Chief
-- Commissioner of Police; (c) an interested party; (d) any other person -- (i) who the Court is satisfied has a sufficient
-- interest ...; and (ii) if the Court is satisfied it is appropriate to give the person leave". (2): if satisfied there are
-- new facts and circumstances, set aside with or without re-opening. (5): "Unless the specified circumstances in
-- subsection (6) apply, a person must not apply ... if an application based on the same, or substantially the same,
-- grounds or evidence had been made and" refused under this section or its 1958 and 1985 predecessors; (6): the refusal
-- was because the earlier provision had been repealed or this section did not then apply. No time limit (finding 6).
@export Can I apply to the Coroners Court to set aside the coroner's findings, and re-open the investigation (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 77)? The senior next of kin, an interested party, or another person with the Court's leave; not again on the same grounds once refused (unless refused only because the old provision had been repealed or did not apply). The Court acts only on new facts and circumstances. No time limit is set; a refusal can be appealed within 90 days (s 84).
GIVEN `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `am I an interested party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were an interested party at the inquest
      `has the court given me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Otherwise: whether the Court is satisfied you have a sufficient interest and it is appropriate to give you leave
      `was the same application refused before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application on the same, or substantially the same, grounds or evidence was refused before (by the Coroners Court, the State Coroner under the 1985 Act, or the Supreme Court under the 1958 or 1985 Acts)
      `was it refused only because the law did not then apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that refusal was only because the earlier provision had been repealed, or this section did not then apply to findings under the old Acts
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to set aside the findings` MEANS
    IF `was the same application refused before` AND (NOT `was it refused only because the law did not then apply`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `am I the senior next of kin` OR `am I an interested party` OR `has the court given me leave`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Appeals to the Supreme Court`

@export May I appeal to the Supreme Court against this coroner's decision (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) Part 7, ss 78 to 85)? Who may appeal depends on the decision: the person who reported the death; the senior next of kin; the person who asked for the autopsy, fire investigation, exhumation, inquest or re-opening, or applied for the body; a person notified of an exhumation; a person with a sufficient interest, or an interested party, for findings.
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision appealed` @desc The decision: not a reportable death (s 78); an autopsy direction (s 79(1)); refusal of an autopsy (s 79(3)); not to investigate a fire (s 80); exhumation authorised (s 81(1)) or refused (s 81(3)); no inquest (s 82); the findings (s 83); refusal to set aside or re-open (s 84); refusal to re-open a discontinued investigation (s 84A); an order releasing a body (s 85)
      `did I report the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reported the death to the coroner
      `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `did I make the request or application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the request or application the decision dealt with (for an autopsy, fire investigation, exhumation, inquest, setting aside or re-opening, or release of the body)
      `was I notified of the exhumation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the State Coroner gave you notice of an exhumation authorisation under s 46(3)
      `do I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a sufficient interest in the investigation
      `was I an interested party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were an interested party at the inquest
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I appeal against the decision` MEANS
    `Part 7 -- I may appeal` `which decision` (`My part in the decision` WITH
        `I reported the death`                           IS `did I report the death`
        `I am the senior next of kin`                    IS `am I the senior next of kin`
        `I made the request or application decided`      IS `did I make the request or application`
        `I was notified of the exhumation authorisation` IS `was I notified of the exhumation`
        `I have a sufficient interest`                   IS `do I have a sufficient interest`
        `I was an interested party at the inquest`       IS `was I an interested party`)

@export By when must I appeal to the Supreme Court against this coroner's decision (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) ss 78 to 84A)? The last day: 28 days after a determination that the death is not reportable or not to investigate a fire; 3 months after a refusal to exhume or a determination not to hold an inquest; 6 months after the findings; 90 days after a refusal to re-open. For autopsy, exhumation-authorisation and body-release decisions the time runs in hours; ask those questions.
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision appealed` @desc The decision you want to appeal against
      `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the coroner, State Coroner or Coroners Court made the decision or refusal
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I appeal` MEANS
    `Part 7 -- the last day to appeal` `which decision` `the day of the decision`

-- The period fixed by the text: on or before the last day, JUST TRUE; after it, JUST FALSE (ruling (a) of 2026-11-05).
@export Is my appeal to the Supreme Court against this coroner's decision in time (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) ss 78 to 84A)? Out of time, the Supreme Court may still give leave in exceptional circumstances (s 86).
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision appealed` @desc The decision you want to appeal against
      `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision or refusal was made
      `the day I appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you institute, or would institute, the appeal
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my appeal in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `Part 7 -- the last day to appeal` `which decision` `the day of the decision`
    WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN (IF `the day I appeal` `is after` `the last day` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 86 (quoted in cor-rules.l4). For ss 79, 81(1), 85 the grant does not reach: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own
-- wording*. The "if" unmet: JUST FALSE.
@export Can the Supreme Court give me leave to appeal out of time against this coroner's decision (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) s 86)? For appeals under ss 78, 80, 81(3), 82, 83, 84 and 84A, if the Court is of the opinion the delay was due to exceptional circumstances and is satisfied leave is desirable in the interests of justice. Not for autopsy, exhumation-authorisation or body-release appeals.
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision appealed` @desc The decision you want to appeal against
      `were there exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is of the opinion that missing the time limit was due to exceptional circumstances
      `is leave in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied that granting leave is desirable in the interests of justice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get leave to appeal out of time` MEANS
    IF NOT (`s 86 -- leave out of time is available for` `which decision`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `were there exceptional circumstances` AND `is leave in the interests of justice` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 87(1) "Subject to section 87A, an appeal to the Supreme Court under this Part is an appeal on a question of law."
-- (1A) includes findings so against the evidence that no reasonable coroner could have made them. s 87A(1) "An appeal ...
-- other than on a question of law may be made under section 82(1) in respect of a decision by a coroner to not hold an
-- inquest into a death, or section 84(1) or 84A(1) in respect of a refusal by the Coroners Court to re-open an
-- investigation into a death, if the appeal is made by -- (a) the senior next of kin of the deceased; or (b) a person with
-- sufficient interest." (2): allowed if necessary or desirable in the interests of justice.
@export Can my appeal to the Supreme Court go beyond a question of law, to the merits (Coroners Act 2008 (Vic) ss 87, 87A)? Only against a decision not to hold an inquest into a death, or a refusal to re-open an investigation into a death, brought by the senior next of kin or a person with a sufficient interest; the Court may allow it if necessary or desirable in the interests of justice. Every other appeal is on a question of law (which includes findings no reasonable coroner could have made on the evidence).
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision appealed` @desc The decision you want to appeal against
      `is it about a death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision concerns a death (not a fire)
      `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin
      `do I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a person with a sufficient interest
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my appeal go beyond a question of law` MEANS
    IF `a decision 87A reaches` AND `is it about a death` AND (`am I the senior next of kin` OR `do I have a sufficient interest`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `a decision 87A reaches` MEANS
            CONSIDER `which decision`
            WHEN `a determination not to hold an inquest (s 82)` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `a refusal to set aside findings or re-open an investigation (s 84)` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `a refusal to re-open a discontinued investigation (s 84A)` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
